 "source_type": "instagram",
 "source_url": "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DdERKdJzpXQ/",
 "title": "Why Gratitude Works Better Than Praising Talent",
 "topic": "communication",
 "summary": "The creator discusses an idea from the book The Courage to Be Disliked regarding praise and hierarchy. Praising someone's skill implicitly places you as their judge, whereas expressing gratitude creates equality by acknowledging what they taught or shared with you.",
 "key_points": [
  "Telling someone 'you're good at this' unconsciously puts you in the position of a judge who decides if they are good enough.",
  "Praising someone's talent creates an unintended hierarchy where one person is above evaluating the person below.",
  "Expressing gratitude replaces judgment with appreciation by acknowledging what the person gave or taught you.",
  "Instead of saying 'you're really good at this', say 'thank you, what you did genuinely taught me something'.",
  "Expressing gratitude keeps both people equal, with no one positioned as superior or inferior."
 ],
 "content": "Post text: Gratitude ♥️ Make a note of this post. Transcript: \"don't appreciate someone who is genuinely talented. I've read this in a book called The Courage to Be Disliked. But at first, I honestly thought it made no sense. Because when someone is really good at something, you naturally want to say you're good at this. But think about what happens underneath those words. When you say you're good at this, you're unconsciously putting yourself in the position of the person who gets to decide whether they're good enough. And the other person can subconsciously register, so I am the one being evaluated. That creates an hierarchy. One person above, one person below. But gratitude changes that completely. Instead of saying you're really good at this, say thank you. What you did genuinely taught me something. Now you're not judging their worth, you're acknowledging what they gave you. No one is above, no one is below, no one is superior, no one is inferior. Just two people who have something valuable to give each other. And that's the kind of appreciation that doesn't put anyone beneath you.\" Text on screen: THE 10 MILLION COPY BESTSELLER THE COURAGE TO BE DISLIKED A single book can change your life ICHIRO KISHIMI AND FUMITAKE KOGA",
